{
  "_meta": {
    "description": "Carbon-cost pass-through coefficients by sector. Pass-through = fraction of carbon cost passed through to customers via prices. The complement (1 - pass_through) is absorbed as direct margin compression on the emitter. Demand elasticity used by Layer 3 network propagation; market_structure is qualitative.",
    "sources": [
      "Sijm, Hers, Lise, Wetzelaer (2012). 'CO2 Cost Pass-Through and Windfall Profits in the Power Sector.' Energy Policy. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.enpol.2007.10.027",
      "Cludius, Hermann, Matthes, Graichen (2020). 'The merit order effect of wind and photovoltaic electricity generation in Germany 2008-2016.' Energy Economics.",
      "Fabra & Reguant (2014). 'Pass-Through of Emissions Costs in Electricity Markets.' American Economic Review 104(9): 2872-99. https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.104.9.2872",
      "Frankovic (2022). 'The impact of carbon pricing in a multi-region production network model.' Bundesbank Discussion Paper 06/2022.",
      "Bauer, Offner, Rudebusch (2023). 'The Macroeconomic Impact of Climate Change: Global vs. Local Temperature.' FRBSF Working Paper.",
      "Devulder & Lisack (2020). 'Carbon tax in a production network.' Banque de France WP #813.",
      "Papageorgiou, Saam, Schulte (2017). 'Substitution between Clean and Dirty Energy Inputs.' REStat 99(2)."
    ],
    "notes": "Pass-through is sector-level median; firm-specific values vary with market power, trade exposure, and contract structure. Demand elasticity is short-run own-price elasticity (Marshallian).",
    "demand_elasticity_note": "REFERENCE ONLY — demand_elasticity is not consumed by the engine. Volume response (pass-through raises price, cuts volume) is a demand judgment handled in the manual layer, not modelled here (Round-1 R4)."
  },
